ypstns is a YP server that answers out of an STNS API server, so that
getpwnam(3), getgrent(3) and everything built on them resolve users and
groups from a directory rather than from master.passwd.

OpenBSD has no nsswitch.conf and no pluggable directory backend of any
other kind.  What it has is YP, which libc has spoken since forever and
which getpwnam(3) consults whenever /etc/master.passwd holds a "+" line.
That is the same reason ypldap(8) is in the base system, and ypstns is
built the same way: a privileged process that holds the maps and answers
the RPC, and an unprivileged one behind unveil(2) and pledge(2) that does
the HTTP and passes finished map entries back over a pipe.

The directory is not served to the network unless it is asked for, and
the master.passwd maps are answered only to a client on a reserved port.

stns-key-wrapper is installed alongside, for sshd_config's
AuthorizedKeysCommand.
